  "question": "What has been proposed to reduce the risk of misleading pandemic forecasts when models are reused for populations unlike those they were trained on, and by what mechanism?",
  "reference_answer": "The proposal is to tailor transferred models to local contexts and develop appropriate model-transfer procedures and guidelines, especially for regions with little data. A model trained elsewhere may fail to account for local cultural norms, demographic characteristics, and behaviors that influence how the virus spreads and affects people. Adapting the model to these differences is intended to make its predictions more appropriate to the destination population, rather than assuming that relationships learned in the original setting apply unchanged.",
  "spans": [
    "Moreover, applying models trained in one context to another raises concerns surrounding the model’s ability to capture aspects such as different cultural norms which may impact the spread and effect of the virus.",
    "In such examples, a transferred model will have to be tailored for local contexts given that there may be different demographic characteristics and behaviors.",
    "Indeed, developing proper model transferability procedures and guidelines is especially important for data poor regions."
  ]
}
